{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Kestrix",
          "version": "0.1.0",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "py.exec.eval",
              "name": "PyExecEval",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "eval() of a value that is not a literal"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "eval runs its argument as Python code. If any part of it comes from a user, a file or the network, an attacker can run arbitrary code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Parse data instead of evaluating it: ast.literal_eval for Python literals, json.loads for JSON, or an explicit lookup table.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** eval runs its argument as Python code. If any part of it comes from a user, a file or the network, an attacker can run arbitrary code.\n\n**Fix:** Parse data instead of evaluating it: ast.literal_eval for Python literals, json.loads for JSON, or an explicit lookup table."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-95"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.exec.exec",
              "name": "PyExecExec",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "exec() of a value that is not a literal"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "exec runs its argument as Python statements, so attacker-influenced text becomes attacker-controlled code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove the dynamic code path. Dispatch to known functions with a dict, or load plugins from a fixed allowlist of modules.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** exec runs its argument as Python statements, so attacker-influenced text becomes attacker-controlled code.\n\n**Fix:** Remove the dynamic code path. Dispatch to known functions with a dict, or load plugins from a fixed allowlist of modules."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-95"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.exec.compile",
              "name": "PyExecCompile",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "compile() of a value that is not a literal"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "compile turns text into a code object that is usually executed next; building it from untrusted text allows code injection."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Avoid compiling dynamic source. If you need an expression language, parse it with ast and evaluate only allowed node types.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** compile turns text into a code object that is usually executed next; building it from untrusted text allows code injection.\n\n**Fix:** Avoid compiling dynamic source. If you need an expression language, parse it with ast and evaluate only allowed node types."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-95"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.subprocess.shell_true",
              "name": "PySubprocessShellTrue",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Command run through a shell"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "With shell=True, or a program list that starts a shell with its command-string option (['sh', '-c', script], ['cmd', '/c', ...], ['powershell', '-Command', ...]), the command is parsed by the shell. Any interpolated value can add ';', '&&' or '$(...)' and run other commands. A command whose values all pass through shlex.quote is reported at low confidence."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pass the command as a list of arguments without shell=True, for example subprocess.run(['git', 'log', ref], check=True). Use shlex.quote only when a shell is unavoidable.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** With shell=True, or a program list that starts a shell with its command-string option (['sh', '-c', script], ['cmd', '/c', ...], ['powershell', '-Command', ...]), the command is parsed by the shell. Any interpolated value can add ';', '&&' or '$(...)' and run other commands. A command whose values all pass through shlex.quote is reported at low confidence.\n\n**Fix:** Pass the command as a list of arguments without shell=True, for example subprocess.run(['git', 'log', ref], check=True). Use shlex.quote only when a shell is unavoidable."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.os.system",
              "name": "PyOsSystem",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "os.system or os.popen with a built command"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "os.system and os.popen always run the string through a shell, so interpolated values can inject commands."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use subprocess.run with a list of arguments and no shell, and check the return code.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** os.system and os.popen always run the string through a shell, so interpolated values can inject commands.\n\n**Fix:** Use subprocess.run with a list of arguments and no shell, and check the return code."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.deserialize.pickle",
              "name": "PyDeserializePickle",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unsafe deserialization (pickle, dill, shelve, marshal, pandas.read_pickle, ZeroMQ recv_pyobj)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Loading a pickle-style stream can run arbitrary code chosen by whoever wrote the bytes. It is never safe on untrusted data. pandas.read_pickle, numpy.load with allow_pickle=True and a ZeroMQ socket's recv_pyobj() all unpickle what they read."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Store and exchange data as JSON (or another data-only format) and validate it, for example with a Pydantic model.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Loading a pickle-style stream can run arbitrary code chosen by whoever wrote the bytes. It is never safe on untrusted data. pandas.read_pickle, numpy.load with allow_pickle=True and a ZeroMQ socket's recv_pyobj() all unpickle what they read.\n\n**Fix:** Store and exchange data as JSON (or another data-only format) and validate it, for example with a Pydantic model."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.deserialize.yaml_unsafe",
              "name": "PyDeserializeYamlUnsafe",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "YAML loaded with a loader that can build objects"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The full and unsafe PyYAML loaders can construct arbitrary Python objects from tags in the document, which leads to code execution."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use yaml.safe_load, or yaml.load(data, Loader=yaml.SafeLoader).",
                "markdown": "**Why:** The full and unsafe PyYAML loaders can construct arbitrary Python objects from tags in the document, which leads to code execution.\n\n**Fix:** Use yaml.safe_load, or yaml.load(data, Loader=yaml.SafeLoader)."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.sql.string_built",
              "name": "PySqlStringBuilt",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQL statement built from strings"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Formatting values into SQL text lets a value close the string and change the query (SQL injection)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use bound parameters: cursor.execute('SELECT * FROM t WHERE id = ?', (item_id,)), or the placeholders of your driver or ORM.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Formatting values into SQL text lets a value close the string and change the query (SQL injection).\n\n**Fix:** Use bound parameters: cursor.execute('SELECT * FROM t WHERE id = ?', (item_id,)), or the placeholders of your driver or ORM."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.tls.verify_disabled",
              "name": "PyTlsVerifyDisabled",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "TLS certificate or hostname verification disabled"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Without verification anyone on the network path can impersonate the server and read or change the traffic."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Keep verification on. For a private CA, pass its bundle (verify='/path/ca.pem' or ssl.create_default_context(cafile=...)).",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Without verification anyone on the network path can impersonate the server and read or change the traffic.\n\n**Fix:** Keep verification on. For a private CA, pass its bundle (verify='/path/ca.pem' or ssl.create_default_context(cafile=...))."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-295"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.crypto.weak_hash",
              "name": "PyCryptoWeakHash",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "MD5 or SHA-1 hash"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "MD5 and SHA-1 have practical collision attacks and must not protect integrity, signatures or passwords."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use hashlib.sha256 (or hashlib.blake2b). For passwords use a slow KDF such as hashlib.scrypt. If the hash is only a checksum or an identifier, pass usedforsecurity=False.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** MD5 and SHA-1 have practical collision attacks and must not protect integrity, signatures or passwords.\n\n**Fix:** Use hashlib.sha256 (or hashlib.blake2b). For passwords use a slow KDF such as hashlib.scrypt. If the hash is only a checksum or an identifier, pass usedforsecurity=False."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-328"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.crypto.insecure_random",
              "name": "PyCryptoInsecureRandom",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "random module used for a secret, cookie or session value"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The random module is a predictable generator. Tokens, keys, salts and passwords made with it, and values it produces that end up in a cookie, the session, a response header or a secret-named argument, can be guessed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use the secrets module: secrets.token_urlsafe(32), secrets.token_hex(16) or secrets.choice.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** The random module is a predictable generator. Tokens, keys, salts and passwords made with it, and values it produces that end up in a cookie, the session, a response header or a secret-named argument, can be guessed.\n\n**Fix:** Use the secrets module: secrets.token_urlsafe(32), secrets.token_hex(16) or secrets.choice."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-330"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.tempfile.mktemp",
              "name": "PyTempfileMktemp",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "tempfile.mktemp returns a name, not a file"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Another process can create the file between mktemp choosing the name and your code opening it, and redirect your writes."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use tempfile.mkstemp, tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile or tempfile.TemporaryDirectory, which create the file atomically.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Another process can create the file between mktemp choosing the name and your code opening it, and redirect your writes.\n\n**Fix:** Use tempfile.mkstemp, tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile or tempfile.TemporaryDirectory, which create the file atomically."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-377"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.flask.debug",
              "name": "PyFlaskDebug",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Debug mode enabled"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Debug mode shows stack traces and, in Flask, an interactive console that runs code for anyone who can reach the page."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Read the setting from the environment and keep it off in deployed code, for example app.run(debug=os.environ.get('FLASK_DEBUG') == '1').",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Debug mode shows stack traces and, in Flask, an interactive console that runs code for anyone who can reach the page.\n\n**Fix:** Read the setting from the environment and keep it off in deployed code, for example app.run(debug=os.environ.get('FLASK_DEBUG') == '1')."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-489"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.jwt.no_verify",
              "name": "PyJwtNoVerify",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "JWT decoded without verifying the signature"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A token whose signature is not checked can be forged by anyone, so its claims (user, role, expiry) mean nothing."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Verify the signature with an explicit algorithm list, for example jwt.decode(token, key, algorithms=['RS256']). Never allow 'none'.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** A token whose signature is not checked can be forged by anyone, so its claims (user, role, expiry) mean nothing.\n\n**Fix:** Verify the signature with an explicit algorithm list, for example jwt.decode(token, key, algorithms=['RS256']). Never allow 'none'."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-347"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.xml.unsafe_parser",
              "name": "PyXmlUnsafeParser",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "XML parser set to resolve external entities"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An XML parser that resolves external entities lets a document read local files or make network requests (XXE): lxml with resolve_entities=True or no_network=False, or a SAX parser (also behind pulldom) with feature_external_ges or feature_external_pes on. The standard-library parsers with their defaults are not reported: xml.etree, minidom, pulldom and sax do not resolve external entities (sax and pulldom since Python 3.7.1), and Expat 2.4.1 or newer resists entity expansion. lxml with huge_tree=True is reported at low severity (CWE-776)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Leave external entities off: etree.XMLParser(resolve_entities=False, no_network=True), and do not turn on the SAX external-entity features. For untrusted XML prefer defusedxml (defusedxml.ElementTree.fromstring), which refuses entities and DTDs, and bound the input size.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** An XML parser that resolves external entities lets a document read local files or make network requests (XXE): lxml with resolve_entities=True or no_network=False, or a SAX parser (also behind pulldom) with feature_external_ges or feature_external_pes on. The standard-library parsers with their defaults are not reported: xml.etree, minidom, pulldom and sax do not resolve external entities (sax and pulldom since Python 3.7.1), and Expat 2.4.1 or newer resists entity expansion. lxml with huge_tree=True is reported at low severity (CWE-776).\n\n**Fix:** Leave external entities off: etree.XMLParser(resolve_entities=False, no_network=True), and do not turn on the SAX external-entity features. For untrusted XML prefer defusedxml (defusedxml.ElementTree.fromstring), which refuses entities and DTDs, and bound the input size."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.assert.security",
              "name": "PyAssertSecurity",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "assert used for an access check"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Python removes assert statements when run with -O, so the check silently disappears in optimized deployments."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use an explicit check: if not user.is_admin: raise PermissionError(...).",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Python removes assert statements when run with -O, so the check silently disappears in optimized deployments.\n\n**Fix:** Use an explicit check: if not user.is_admin: raise PermissionError(...)."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-617"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.path.traversal",
              "name": "PyPathTraversal",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "File path built from request data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A value such as '../../etc/passwd' in a request parameter can escape the intended directory and read or overwrite other files."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Reduce the name with werkzeug.utils.secure_filename or os.path.basename, or resolve the path and check it stays under the base directory (Path.resolve().is_relative_to(base)). Flask's send_from_directory does this for you.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** A value such as '../../etc/passwd' in a request parameter can escape the intended directory and read or overwrite other files.\n\n**Fix:** Reduce the name with werkzeug.utils.secure_filename or os.path.basename, or resolve the path and check it stays under the base directory (Path.resolve().is_relative_to(base)). Flask's send_from_directory does this for you."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-22"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.ssrf.request",
              "name": "PySsrfRequest",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "HTTP request to a URL taken from request data or model output"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When a user (or a model's output) chooses the URL, the server can be made to call internal services, cloud metadata endpoints or other hosts on the attacker's behalf."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Fix the scheme and host in code and let the input choose only a path or query value, or check the parsed host against an allowlist before the request. Do not follow redirects to other hosts.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** When a user (or a model's output) chooses the URL, the server can be made to call internal services, cloud metadata endpoints or other hosts on the attacker's behalf.\n\n**Fix:** Fix the scheme and host in code and let the input choose only a path or query value, or check the parsed host against an allowlist before the request. Do not follow redirects to other hosts."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-918"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.redirect.open",
              "name": "PyRedirectOpen",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Redirect to a location taken from request data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A redirect target taken from the request lets a link on your domain send users to a phishing site that looks trusted."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Redirect to a path you build (url_for, reverse) or check the target first: allow only relative paths on your own host, for example with Django's url_has_allowed_host_and_scheme.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** A redirect target taken from the request lets a link on your domain send users to a phishing site that looks trusted.\n\n**Fix:** Redirect to a path you build (url_for, reverse) or check the target first: allow only relative paths on your own host, for example with Django's url_has_allowed_host_and_scheme."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-601"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.xss.markup",
              "name": "PyXssMarkup",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Request data or rendered Markdown marked as safe HTML, or request data used as a template"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Markup and mark_safe switch off autoescaping, so request data in them is rendered as HTML and script (XSS). The output of a Markdown renderer is the same: Markdown passes raw HTML through, so marking it safe without a sanitizer lets a script tag in the source text run. As the source of render_template_string it is a template, and template syntax in it runs on the server (SSTI, CWE-1336)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pass the value to the template as data and let autoescaping handle it, or escape it first with markupsafe.escape or html.escape. For Markdown, clean the rendered HTML with bleach.clean or nh3.clean before marking it safe. Never build a template's source from input; use render_template with a fixed template file.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Markup and mark_safe switch off autoescaping, so request data in them is rendered as HTML and script (XSS). The output of a Markdown renderer is the same: Markdown passes raw HTML through, so marking it safe without a sanitizer lets a script tag in the source text run. As the source of render_template_string it is a template, and template syntax in it runs on the server (SSTI, CWE-1336).\n\n**Fix:** Pass the value to the template as data and let autoescaping handle it, or escape it first with markupsafe.escape or html.escape. For Markdown, clean the rendered HTML with bleach.clean or nh3.clean before marking it safe. Never build a template's source from input; use render_template with a fixed template file."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.llm.output_to_sink",
              "name": "PyLlmOutputToSink",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "LLM output used as code, a shell command, SQL or a file path"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A model's output follows whatever text reached its prompt. A prompt injection hidden in a web page, a document or a user message can make the model emit code, a command, SQL or a path, and this sink runs it with your program's permissions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Treat model output as untrusted input. Parse it into a fixed structure (JSON validated against a schema, or a choice from an allowlist), pass values as arguments rather than code or command text, and use bound SQL parameters and basename-checked paths.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** A model's output follows whatever text reached its prompt. A prompt injection hidden in a web page, a document or a user message can make the model emit code, a command, SQL or a path, and this sink runs it with your program's permissions.\n\n**Fix:** Treat model output as untrusted input. Parse it into a fixed structure (JSON validated against a schema, or a choice from an allowlist), pass values as arguments rather than code or command text, and use bound SQL parameters and basename-checked paths."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.ml.unsafe_model_load",
              "name": "PyMlUnsafeModelLoad",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Model loaded in a way that can run code from the file"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Pickle-based model files (torch.load without weights_only, joblib, pickle) and Keras files with safe_mode=False can run arbitrary code when loaded, and trust_remote_code=True runs Python shipped with a downloaded model. A tampered or malicious model file is enough."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Load weights only: torch.load(path, weights_only=True), or safetensors. Keep Keras safe_mode on. Load joblib or pickle models only from files you produced and verify their hash. Leave trust_remote_code off, or pin the model revision you reviewed.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Pickle-based model files (torch.load without weights_only, joblib, pickle) and Keras files with safe_mode=False can run arbitrary code when loaded, and trust_remote_code=True runs Python shipped with a downloaded model. A tampered or malicious model file is enough.\n\n**Fix:** Load weights only: torch.load(path, weights_only=True), or safetensors. Keep Keras safe_mode on. Load joblib or pickle models only from files you produced and verify their hash. Leave trust_remote_code off, or pin the model revision you reviewed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.xss.reflected",
              "name": "PyXssReflected",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "HTML response built from request data without escaping"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A view that returns an f-string or concatenation with markup and request data (a Flask view's str, Response, make_response, Django HttpResponse or FastAPI HTMLResponse) sends that data as HTML, so a script in it runs in the visitor's browser (reflected XSS)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Render a template with autoescaping (render_template, Django templates, Jinja2 with autoescape on), or escape each value first with markupsafe.escape, django.utils.html.escape or html.escape. Return JSON instead of HTML when the client does not need markup.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** A view that returns an f-string or concatenation with markup and request data (a Flask view's str, Response, make_response, Django HttpResponse or FastAPI HTMLResponse) sends that data as HTML, so a script in it runs in the visitor's browser (reflected XSS).\n\n**Fix:** Render a template with autoescaping (render_template, Django templates, Jinja2 with autoescape on), or escape each value first with markupsafe.escape, django.utils.html.escape or html.escape. Return JSON instead of HTML when the client does not need markup."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.ldap.injection",
              "name": "PyLdapInjection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "LDAP search filter built from request data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Characters such as '*', '(', ')' and '\\' in a value formatted into an LDAP filter change the filter: '*)(uid=*' matches every entry, which can bypass a login check or list the directory."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Escape each value with ldap.filter.escape_filter_chars (python-ldap) or ldap3.utils.conv.escape_filter_chars, or build the filter with ldap.filter.filter_format(template, [value]).",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Characters such as '*', '(', ')' and '\\' in a value formatted into an LDAP filter change the filter: '*)(uid=*' matches every entry, which can bypass a login check or list the directory.\n\n**Fix:** Escape each value with ldap.filter.escape_filter_chars (python-ldap) or ldap3.utils.conv.escape_filter_chars, or build the filter with ldap.filter.filter_format(template, [value])."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-90"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.xpath.injection",
              "name": "PyXpathInjection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "XPath expression built from request data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A quote in a value formatted into an XPath expression ends the string literal, so the rest of the value becomes XPath: \"' or '1'='1\" selects every node. It applies to lxml xpath() and XPath(), and to ElementTree find/findall paths."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pass values as XPath variables instead of formatting them in: tree.xpath('//user[name=$name]', name=name), or etree.XPath('//user[name=$name]'). With ElementTree, iterate and compare in Python: [e for e in root.iter('user') if e.get('name') == name].",
                "markdown": "**Why:** A quote in a value formatted into an XPath expression ends the string literal, so the rest of the value becomes XPath: \"' or '1'='1\" selects every node. It applies to lxml xpath() and XPath(), and to ElementTree find/findall paths.\n\n**Fix:** Pass values as XPath variables instead of formatting them in: tree.xpath('//user[name=$name]', name=name), or etree.XPath('//user[name=$name]'). With ElementTree, iterate and compare in Python: [e for e in root.iter('user') if e.get('name') == name]."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-643"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.cookie.insecure",
              "name": "PyCookieInsecure",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Cookie set without the Secure or HttpOnly flag"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Without Secure the browser also sends the cookie over plain HTTP, where anyone on the network can read it. Without HttpOnly any script in the page, including an injected one, can read it. Flags set to False explicitly are medium severity; flags left out are reported at low severity. Only HttpOnly missing is reported as CWE-1004."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pass both flags: response.set_cookie('name', value, secure=True, httponly=True, samesite='Lax'). Leave httponly off only for a cookie that the page's own JavaScript must read and that holds nothing secret.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Without Secure the browser also sends the cookie over plain HTTP, where anyone on the network can read it. Without HttpOnly any script in the page, including an injected one, can read it. Flags set to False explicitly are medium severity; flags left out are reported at low severity. Only HttpOnly missing is reported as CWE-1004.\n\n**Fix:** Pass both flags: response.set_cookie('name', value, secure=True, httponly=True, samesite='Lax'). Leave httponly off only for a cookie that the page's own JavaScript must read and that holds nothing secret."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-614"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.trust_boundary.session",
              "name": "PyTrustBoundarySession",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Request data stored in the session without validation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Code that reads the session trusts it as data the server wrote. A request value copied into session[...] without a check (an allowlist, a type conversion) mixes untrusted and trusted data, and later code may treat it as verified, for example as the user's role."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Validate the value before storing it: check it against an allowlist (if role not in ROLES: abort(400)), convert it (int(value)), or store only values the server derived itself.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Code that reads the session trusts it as data the server wrote. A request value copied into session[...] without a check (an allowlist, a type conversion) mixes untrusted and trusted data, and later code may treat it as verified, for example as the user's role.\n\n**Fix:** Validate the value before storing it: check it against an allowlist (if role not in ROLES: abort(400)), convert it (int(value)), or store only values the server derived itself."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-501"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.archive.extract",
              "name": "PyArchiveExtract",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Tar archive extracted without a member filter"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A tar archive can hold members named ../../etc/cron.d/x, absolute paths or links that point outside the target directory. extract and extractall write them wherever the name says unless the members are filtered, so an archive from outside the program can overwrite files (CVE-2007-4559). shutil.unpack_archive extracts tar archives the same way."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pass filter='data' (Python 3.12 and the security releases of 3.8 to 3.11; shutil.unpack_archive takes it too) so extraction refuses paths that leave the directory, or check each member's resolved path with os.path.commonpath before passing the safe members to extractall.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** A tar archive can hold members named ../../etc/cron.d/x, absolute paths or links that point outside the target directory. extract and extractall write them wherever the name says unless the members are filtered, so an archive from outside the program can overwrite files (CVE-2007-4559). shutil.unpack_archive extracts tar archives the same way.\n\n**Fix:** Pass filter='data' (Python 3.12 and the security releases of 3.8 to 3.11; shutil.unpack_archive takes it too) so extraction refuses paths that leave the directory, or check each member's resolved path with os.path.commonpath before passing the safe members to extractall."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "low",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-22"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.xml.stdlib_parse",
              "name": "PyXmlStdlibParse",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Standard-library XML parser given a string, in a file that handles network data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "xml.etree, minidom, pulldom and sax parse a document, including its entity definitions, without a size or expansion limit of their own. Python's \"XML vulnerabilities\" table lists quadratic blowup as a risk of all of them, and older Expat releases also expand nested entities (billion laughs). They do not read external entities by default. Reported, at low severity, when fromstring, XML or parseString gets a value that is not a literal in a file that imports a web framework or an HTTP or socket library (requests, httpx, urllib, aiohttp, http, socket), where the text is likely to come from outside. Not reported: parse() of a file, and any file that imports defusedxml."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Parse untrusted XML with defusedxml (defusedxml.ElementTree.fromstring), and bound the input size before parsing.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** xml.etree, minidom, pulldom and sax parse a document, including its entity definitions, without a size or expansion limit of their own. Python's \"XML vulnerabilities\" table lists quadratic blowup as a risk of all of them, and older Expat releases also expand nested entities (billion laughs). They do not read external entities by default. Reported, at low severity, when fromstring, XML or parseString gets a value that is not a literal in a file that imports a web framework or an HTTP or socket library (requests, httpx, urllib, aiohttp, http, socket), where the text is likely to come from outside. Not reported: parse() of a file, and any file that imports defusedxml.\n\n**Fix:** Parse untrusted XML with defusedxml (defusedxml.ElementTree.fromstring), and bound the input size before parsing."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "precision": "low",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-776"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "py.xss.autoescape_off",
              "name": "PyXssAutoescapeOff",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Jinja2 Environment created without autoescape"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Jinja2's Environment does not escape rendered values unless autoescape is on, so a user-supplied value in an HTML template becomes cross-site scripting. Reported, at low severity, when autoescape is left out and the Environment loads template files (FileSystemLoader, PackageLoader) or the file imports a web framework. An explicit autoescape argument, or **options that may hold one, is not reported."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pass autoescape=True, or jinja2.select_autoescape(['html', 'xml']) so HTML templates are escaped and plain-text ones are not.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Jinja2's Environment does not escape rendered values unless autoescape is on, so a user-supplied value in an HTML template becomes cross-site scripting. Reported, at low severity, when autoescape is left out and the Environment loads template files (FileSystemLoader, PackageLoader) or the file imports a web framework. An explicit autoescape argument, or **options that may hold one, is not reported.\n\n**Fix:** Pass autoescape=True, or jinja2.select_autoescape(['html', 'xml']) so HTML templates are escaped and plain-text ones are not."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.0",
                "precision": "low",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "config.claude.hook_remote_exec",
              "name": "ConfigClaudeHookRemoteExec",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Claude Code hook runs a script downloaded at run time"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Hooks in project settings and plugins run shell commands on the developer's machine. A hook that pipes curl or wget into a shell runs whatever the server returns that day, with no review and no integrity check; a malicious repository can use this to run code when the project is opened (Check Point, CVE-2025-59536)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Commit the script to the repository (or the plugin) and run that file, or download a pinned version and verify its SHA-256 before running it. Review hooks before trusting a project.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Hooks in project settings and plugins run shell commands on the developer's machine. A hook that pipes curl or wget into a shell runs whatever the server returns that day, with no review and no integrity check; a malicious repository can use this to run code when the project is opened (Check Point, CVE-2025-59536).\n\n**Fix:** Commit the script to the repository (or the plugin) and run that file, or download a pinned version and verify its SHA-256 before running it. Review hooks before trusting a project."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-494"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "config.claude.permissions_bypass",
              "name": "ConfigClaudePermissionsBypass",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Project settings skip Claude Code permission prompts"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "defaultMode bypassPermissions, an allow rule for every Bash command, or enableAllProjectMcpServers lets the agent run any command or project MCP server without asking. In a shared project file it applies to everyone who opens the repository, including a repository an attacker prepared."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove the setting from project files. Allow specific commands, such as Bash(npm run test *), and approve MCP servers one by one. Use bypassPermissions only in a disposable container or VM, set from user settings or the command line.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** defaultMode bypassPermissions, an allow rule for every Bash command, or enableAllProjectMcpServers lets the agent run any command or project MCP server without asking. In a shared project file it applies to everyone who opens the repository, including a repository an attacker prepared.\n\n**Fix:** Remove the setting from project files. Allow specific commands, such as Bash(npm run test *), and approve MCP servers one by one. Use bypassPermissions only in a disposable container or VM, set from user settings or the command line."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-250"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "config.mcp.unpinned_launcher",
              "name": "ConfigMcpUnpinnedLauncher",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "MCP server launched from a package without a pinned version"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "npx -y and uvx download and run the newest release of the package at every start. If the package or its publisher account is compromised, the next start runs the attacker's code with access to your files and tokens."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pin an exact version, for example npx -y some-mcp-server@1.4.2 or uvx some-mcp-server==1.4.2, and update it deliberately after reviewing the release.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** npx -y and uvx download and run the newest release of the package at every start. If the package or its publisher account is compromised, the next start runs the attacker's code with access to your files and tokens.\n\n**Fix:** Pin an exact version, for example npx -y some-mcp-server@1.4.2 or uvx some-mcp-server==1.4.2, and update it deliberately after reviewing the release."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-829"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "config.mcp.secret_inline",
              "name": "ConfigMcpSecretInline",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Credential written into .mcp.json"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": ".mcp.json is meant to be committed and shared, so a key or token in a server's env, args or headers is visible to everyone with the repository and stays in its history."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Reference an environment variable instead, for example \"GITHUB_TOKEN\": \"${GITHUB_TOKEN}\", and set it in your shell or a secrets manager. Rotate the credential if it was ever committed.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** .mcp.json is meant to be committed and shared, so a key or token in a server's env, args or headers is visible to everyone with the repository and stays in its history.\n\n**Fix:** Reference an environment variable instead, for example \"GITHUB_TOKEN\": \"${GITHUB_TOKEN}\", and set it in your shell or a secrets manager. Rotate the credential if it was ever committed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
